{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01K4H5f9T7dZPBpwPaSQxSWe","type":"tool_result","content":"1\t# Report: Batch MLE Interpolation\n2\t\n3\t## Description\n4\t\n5\tReplace per-AIR `interpolate_columns_gpu` kernel launches in MLE sumcheck rounds with a single batched descriptor-array kernel launch per round. Each MLE round previously iterated over ~310 AIRs (at APC 300), issuing a separate `cudaMallocAsync` + H2D column-pointer upload + kernel launch per AIR. Nsys profiling showed 4993 total `interpolate_columns_kernel` launches with only 20ms GPU compute time, suggesting that allocation and launch overhead dominates the 180ms MLE Rounds span. The batched approach consolidates these into one large allocation, two H2D uploads (descriptors + column pointers), and one kernel launch per round.\n6\t\n7\t## Implementation\n8\t\n9\t### Files changed\n10\t\n11\t1. **`crates/cuda-backend/cuda/src/logup_zerocheck/utils.cu`**:\n12\t   - Added `InterpColDesc` struct with `output`, `columns_offset`, `num_y`, `num_columns`, `total_threads`, and `block_start` fields\n13\t   - Added `batched_interpolate_columns_kernel` — a multi-block-per-descriptor kernel using binary search to map each CUDA block to its descriptor. Each thread handles one (y, col) pair, identical computation to the original kernel.\n14\t   - Added `_batched_interpolate_columns` launcher that launches `total_blocks` CUDA blocks\n15\t\n16\t2. **`crates/cuda-backend/src/cuda/logup_zerocheck.rs`**:\n17\t   - Added Rust `InterpColDesc` struct (`#[repr(C)]`) matching the CUDA layout\n18\t   - Added FFI extern declaration and safe wrapper `batched_interpolate_columns_gpu`\n19\t\n20\t3. **`crates/cuda-backend/src/logup_zerocheck/mod.rs`**:\n21\t   - Restructured `sumcheck_polys_batch_eval` into three phases:\n22\t     - **Phase 1**: CPU-only metadata collection — iterates all traces, splits Case A (late eval) vs Case B (early eval). For Case B traces, collects column pointers and sizing info into flat arrays without any CUDA calls.\n23\t     - **Phase 2**: Single big `DeviceBuffer<EF>` allocation for all interpolated data + descriptor/column-pointer upload + single batched kernel launch\n24\t     - **Phase 3**: Build `TraceCtx` for each Case B trace using pointer offsets into the big buffer. Per-trace `main_ptrs.to_device()` calls remain (see below).\n25\t\n26\t### Deviations from plan\n27\t\n28\tThe original plan used a one-block-per-trace kernel design. Initial testing showed this caused severe GPU underutilization for large AIRs (APC 0 regressed from 119ms to 198ms) because large traces with 50K+ threads were constrained to a single block of 512 threads. The kernel was redesigned to a multi-block-per-descriptor approach using binary search, where each descriptor receives `ceil(total_threads / 512)` blocks. This fixed the regression completely.\n29\t\n30\t### Key decisions\n31\t\n32\t- **Binary search in kernel**: Each block performs a binary search over descriptors (O(log N), ~9 comparisons for 310 descriptors) to find its owning descriptor. This adds minimal overhead vs a lookup table approach while avoiding an extra H2D upload.\n33\t- **Residual `main_ptrs.to_device()` not batched**: The plan correctly noted that per-trace `main_ptrs.to_device()` calls (~5000 per benchmark) remain. Batching these would require changing the `TraceCtx.main_ptrs_dev` type from `DeviceBuffer<MainMatrixPtrs<EF>>` to a raw pointer, cascading through all evaluator code.\n34\t\n35\t## Results\n36\t\n37\t### APC 0\n38\t\n39\t| Metric | Baseline | Before Task | After Task | vs Baseline | vs Before |\n40\t|--------|----------|-------------|------------|-------------|-----------|\n41\t| STARK excl trace | 2153ms | 2172ms | 2160ms | +7ms, 1.00x | -12ms, 1.01x lower |\n42\t| Constraints | 1292ms | 1336ms | 1330ms | +38ms, 1.03x higher | -6ms, 1.00x |\n43\t| MLE Rounds | 118ms | 119ms | 114ms | -4ms, 1.04x lower | -5ms, 1.04x lower |\n44\t| Round 0 | 178ms | 178ms | 178ms | 0ms, 1.00x | 0ms, 1.00x |\n45\t| LogUp GKR | 993ms | 1035ms | 1033ms | +40ms, 1.04x higher | -2ms, 1.00x |\n46\t| Trace Commit | 518ms | 532ms | 528ms | +10ms, 1.02x higher | -4ms, 1.01x lower |\n47\t\n48\t### APC 100\n49\t\n50\t| Metric | Baseline | Before Task | After Task | vs Baseline | vs Before |\n51\t|--------|----------|-------------|------------|-------------|-----------|\n52\t| STARK excl trace | 2155ms | 1605ms | 1589ms | -566ms, 1.36x lower | -16ms, 1.01x lower |\n53\t| Constraints | 1393ms | 1046ms | 1038ms | -355ms, 1.34x lower | -8ms, 1.01x lower |\n54\t| MLE Rounds | 150ms | 150ms | 139ms | -11ms, 1.08x lower | -11ms, 1.08x lower |\n55\t| Round 0 | 464ms | 252ms | 242ms | -222ms, 1.92x lower | -10ms, 1.04x lower |\n56\t| LogUp GKR | 775ms | 641ms | 653ms | -122ms, 1.19x lower | +12ms, 1.02x higher |\n57\t| Trace Commit | 418ms | 344ms | 340ms | -78ms, 1.23x lower | -4ms, 1.01x lower |\n58\t\n59\t### APC 300\n60\t\n61\t| Metric | Baseline | Before Task | After Task | vs Baseline | vs Before |\n62\t|--------|----------|-------------|------------|-------------|-----------|\n63\t| STARK excl trace | 2455ms | 1441ms | 1418ms | -1037ms, 1.73x lower | -23ms, 1.02x lower |\n64\t| Constraints | 1634ms | 1015ms | 991ms | -643ms, 1.65x lower | -24ms, 1.02x lower |\n65\t| MLE Rounds | 180ms | 183ms | 168ms | -12ms, 1.07x lower | -15ms, 1.09x lower |\n66\t| Round 0 | 662ms | 296ms | 298ms | -364ms, 2.22x lower | +2ms, 1.01x higher |\n67\t| LogUp GKR | 790ms | 534ms | 521ms | -269ms, 1.52x lower | -13ms, 1.02x lower |\n68\t| Trace Commit | 406ms | 247ms | 250ms | -156ms, 1.62x lower | +3ms, 1.01x higher |\n69\t| Stacked Reduction | 311ms | 75ms | 74ms | -237ms, 4.20x lower | -1ms, 1.01x lower |\n70\t\n71\t### Stability at APC 300 (5 after-runs)\n72\t\n73\tMLE Rounds: 168, 169, 170, 171, 168ms (mean: 169ms, std: ±1.3ms)\n74\tSTARK excl trace: 1412, 1415, 1425, 1418ms (mean: 1418ms, std: ±5ms)\n75\t\n76\t## Assessment\n77\t\n78\tThe optimization achieved a **consistent but modest improvement**:\n79\t\n80\t- **MLE Rounds at APC 300**: 183ms → 168ms (**15ms, 8.2% improvement**). This is below the plan's 20ms rollback threshold but well above measurement noise (5 runs show ±1.3ms variance). The improvement is real.\n81\t- **STARK excl trace at APC 300**: 1441ms → 1418ms (**23ms, 1.6% improvement**). This exceeds the plan's 15ms threshold.\n82\t- **MLE Rounds at APC 100**: 150ms → 139ms (**11ms, 7.3% improvement**).\n83\t- **No regression at APC 0**: STARK excl trace -12ms (noise), MLE Rounds -5ms.\n84\t\n85\tThe improvement is smaller than the plan's optimistic estimate (30-50ms) because:\n86\t\n87\t1. **Residual per-trace `main_ptrs.to_device()` overhead**: ~5000 calls remain, each requiring `cudaMallocAsync` + `cudaMemcpyAsync`. This is likely ~50-75ms of overhead that the optimization does not address.\n88\t2. **Per-call CUDA API overhead is lower than estimated**: The plan assumed 15μs per API call. Modern CUDA pool allocators (`cudaMallocAsync` from virtual memory pools) have sub-5μs overhead, making the eliminated overhead smaller than expected.\n89\t3. **The MLE Rounds span includes evaluation time**: Not all 183ms was interpolation overhead — the span also covers downstream `evaluate_logup_batched` and `evaluate_zerocheck_batched` calls.\n90\t\n91\t**Cumulative STARK excl trace improvement vs baseline**: 1.73x at APC 300 (up from 1.70x before this task).\n92\t\n93\t## Future Work\n94\t\n95\t- **Batch `main_ptrs.to_device()` calls**: The ~5000 per-benchmark `main_ptrs.to_device()` calls are the largest remaining overhead source in MLE Rounds. Batching these would require changing `TraceCtx.main_ptrs_dev` from `DeviceBuffer<MainMatrixPtrs<EF>>` to a raw pointer offset into a shared buffer, cascading through evaluator code. This is the clear next step for further MLE Rounds improvement.\n96\t- **Pre-allocate the big interpolated buffer**: Currently each round allocates a fresh big buffer via `DeviceBuffer::with_capacity`. Pre-computing the max buffer size and reusing a single allocation across rounds would eliminate the per-round `cudaMallocAsync`.\n97\t- **Profile remaining MLE Rounds overhead**: After batching main_ptrs, profile to identify whether the remaining ~100ms is GPU compute, H2D copies, or CPU-side TraceCtx construction.\n98\t- **Combine with multi-stream parallelism**: The MLE Rounds path runs on a single CUDA stream. If per-trace evaluations can be distributed across streams (like Round 0 and GKR), concurrent kernel execution could further reduce wall time.\n99\t"}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"50e92e47-0cd3-4f35-91c1-5f23da5f72d3","uuid":"a89f6c7d-ffd6-4809-84b7-9d8e8affc362","timestamp":"2026-04-13T20:43:54.660Z","tool_use_result":{"type":"text","file":{"filePath":"/home/georg/stark-backend/autoopt-results/2026-04-13-1100-batch-mle-interpolation/report.md","content":"# Report: Batch MLE Interpolation\n\n## Description\n\nReplace per-AIR `interpolate_columns_gpu` kernel launches in MLE sumcheck rounds with a single batched descriptor-array kernel launch per round. Each MLE round previously iterated over ~310 AIRs (at APC 300), issuing a separate `cudaMallocAsync` + H2D column-pointer upload + kernel launch per AIR. Nsys profiling showed 4993 total `interpolate_columns_kernel` launches with only 20ms GPU compute time, suggesting that allocation and launch overhead dominates the 180ms MLE Rounds span. The batched approach consolidates these into one large allocation, two H2D uploads (descriptors + column pointers), and one kernel launch per round.\n\n## Implementation\n\n### Files changed\n\n1. **`crates/cuda-backend/cuda/src/logup_zerocheck/utils.cu`**:\n   - Added `InterpColDesc` struct with `output`, `columns_offset`, `num_y`, `num_columns`, `total_threads`, and `block_start` fields\n   - Added `batched_interpolate_columns_kernel` — a multi-block-per-descriptor kernel using binary search to map each CUDA block to its descriptor. Each thread handles one (y,